#: {{syn|en|nouveau}}
# Having done something a short while ago that distinguishes them as what they are called.
#: {{ux|en|The cause has several hundred '''recent''' donors.}}
#: {{ux|en|I met three '''recent''' graduates at the conference.}}
# {{lb|en|sciences}} Particularly in geology, palaeontology, and astronomy: having occurred a relatively short time ago, but still potentially thousands or even millions of years ago.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 4 2020|author=Evan Gough|titleurl=https://www.sciencealert.com/a-supernova-exploded-dangerously-close-to-earth-2-5-million-years-ago|title=We Now Have Proof a Supernova Exploded Perilously Close to Earth 2.5 Million Years Ago|journal=Science Alert
|passage=Finding it now means it was produced in more '''recent''' times, in astronomical terms.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261006145635/https://www.sciencealert.com/a-supernova-exploded-dangerously-close-to-earth-2-5-million-years-ago|archivedate=6 October 2026}}

